      "title": "2. Possibility, comparison and uncertainty",
      "text": "**Original:** “Even a technique that raises output may not be commercially viable if transport is expensive or buyers offer low prices.”\n\n**Plain English:** Producing more is not enough to make a method financially workable when delivery costs are high or selling prices are low.\n\n**Structure:** **Even** + noun phrase with a relative clause + modal predicate + conditional clause containing two alternatives.\n\n**Main clause:** “Even a technique … may not be commercially viable.”\n\n**Dependent clauses:** “that raises output” modifies *technique*. “If transport is expensive or buyers offer low prices” supplies the conditions; its two finite clauses are joined by *or*.\n\n**Important grammar:** *Even* signals a surprising limit. *May not* expresses possible failure; it does not mean definite failure. The present tense after *if* describes a general condition.\n\n**Vocabulary / collocation:** raise output; commercially viable; offer low prices.\n\n**Academic effect:** A concession is linked to an economic mechanism, showing why one positive indicator may not determine the overall result.\n\n**New example:** “Even a service that saves time may not be financially viable if operating costs are high or demand remains weak.”",
