      "text": "**Original:** “If a programme is to scale up, evidence about ordinary delivery conditions matters more than its most impressive showcase.”\n\n**Plain English:** Before expanding a programme, we need to know how it works normally, rather than rely mainly on its best example.\n\n**Structure:** Initial conditional clause + main clause with a comparison.\n\n**Main clause:** “Evidence about ordinary delivery conditions matters more than its most impressive showcase.”\n\n**Dependent clause:** “If a programme is to scale up.” The comparison after *than* is compressed: it can be expanded to “than its most impressive showcase matters.”\n\n**Important grammar:** **Be to + infinitive** here points to an intended future outcome. In the conditional, it introduces what needs consideration for that outcome to occur. The singular head *evidence* takes **matters**; the nearby plural *conditions* does not control agreement.\n\n**Vocabulary / collocation:** scale up; delivery conditions; impressive showcase.\n\n**Academic effect:** The structure connects a proposed action to a criterion for judging whether it is justified.\n\n**New example:** “If an online service is to expand, evidence about everyday reliability matters more than a polished demonstration.”",
